  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Severe thunderstorms are possible Thursday amidst\nincreasingly warm and humid conditions.\n\nCurrent surface analysis depicts a warm front still to our south\nover the VA/NC border, as well as a cold front over the Ohio\nValley extending southward from the parent low in Quebec. \nAloft, latest satellite imagery depicts an influx of moisture \ninto the region this morning in response to a subtle jetlet \nwithin the main trough axis. This is producing some showers to \nour northeast of southeastern PA and into NJ as well. This \nactivity is building slowly southwest this morning, and is still\nexpected to build into northeast MD in the next several hours \nleading up to sunrise. \n\nMore notable forcing arrives later today as the main upper \nlevel trough and surface cold front approach from the northwest.\nHowever, these forcing features may be somewhat displaced from \nthe local area during peak diurnal heating. The front will lag \nto our northwest, while the strongest forcing with the trough \npasses to the north. A secondary shortwave may approach but not \nuntil the evening. Thus there remains some uncertainty in the \ncoverage and timing of any showers and thunderstorms, although \nthere is some model consensus on development along a surface \ntrough between the I-95 and I-81 corridors by mid afternoon. \nResidual cloud cover and any leftover morning showers could put \na damper on instability. Then, especially for areas near and \neast of I-95, convection may not arrive until after sunset, when\nit could be weakening. However, if everything does come \ntogether, moderate instability combined with deep-layer shear of\naround 25-35 kt could result in some severe thunderstorms. \nDamaging winds are the primary threat. Moderate mid level lapse \nrates, strong winds in the upper atmosphere, and relatively \nlight low level winds could also result in some severe level \nhail. As mentioned in previous forecasts, model guidance is\nstill depicting some decent low-level curvature to hodographs,\nespecially along the MD/PA border in northeast MD. The Storm\nPrediction Center has actually added this area in to a two-\npercent tornado threat today, which makes a lot of sense, should\nthings all come together. We will have to monitor heavy rain \npotential as well, since relatively unidirectional flow relative\nto the surface trough could result in some training (hinted at \nby splotches of higher QPF in some CAMs). At the moment, this \nthreat appears higher to our north, and precipitable water \nvalues and warm cloud depths are relatively modest. Thus no \nFlood Watches are planned at this time. While some showers and \nstorms may linger into the night, the severe weather threat \nshould be winding down by mid to late evening.\n\nThe cold front may bisect the area by Friday morning while the\nmid/upper level trough axis remains to the west. Some showers\nand thunderstorms will continue to be possible near and south of\nthe front, although the risk for strong thunderstorms is lower.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Turning drier this weekend, with increasingly hot \nand humid conditions for early/mid next week.\n\nThe late week cold front is expected to stall along the southeastern \nU.S. coastline throughout the weekend. As this occurs, broad surface \nhigh pressure is likely to extend from the southern Mid-Atlantic up \nto New England. This configuration will support tranquil weather \nthis weekend with seasonable temperatures. Mid-level heights (585 to \n589 dm) remain slightly above average which does keep temperatures \non the warmer side. Humidity levels drop a bit to start the weekend, \nbut do begin to come up some as winds shift to southerly on the back \nside of the exiting surface ridge. This eventually sets up for a hot \nand humid period into next week which also marks the conclusion of \nMeteorological Summer (June 1-August 31).\n\nA persistent mid/upper ridge across the southern tier of the country \nis forecast to flex northward in time. Global ensembles generally \nagree on this evolution, but vary with details along the northern \nedge of this ridge. The net result will be another round of hot \ntemperatures along with increasing humidity. During the early to mid-\nweek period, forecast highs rise into the low/mid 90s, with heat \nindices between 98 and 103 degrees. This could inch closer to the \n105 degree heat index mark by Tuesday and Wednesday which is Heat \nAdvisory criteria for locations east of the Blue Ridge. Despite all \nof the heat and humidity, daily thunderstorm chances could be more \nlimited if the area is underneath the upper ridge. However, these \ndetails will likely become better resolved in a few days.\n\nLooking a bit further ahead, the Day 6 to 10 outlook from the \nClimate Prediction Center favors above average chances for continued \nheat to linger into subsequent days.",
